<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diwali</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dipavali, Deepavali, Dīpāvalī</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diwali, or Dipavali, is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> festival of lights. It is celebrated over five or six days between around mid-October and mid-November and is associated with the triumph of light over darkness and good over evil; Hindus connect it with the day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> returned to his kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> after defeating the demon king Ravana, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a>, the goddess of prosperity. Jains mark the final liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> the release of Guru Hargobind from a Mughal prison. The festival originated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, likely as a combination of harvest festivals of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Celebrants light their homes and temples with diyas, oil lamps.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diwali.jpg" alt="Diwali" width="330" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Subharnab Majumdar · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Rangoli_of_Lights.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pilgrimage to sacred rivers and cities such as Varanasi on the Ganges, and festivals such as <b>Diwali</b>, the festival of lights, and Holi, the festival of colours, mark the year.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
